Interesting. You write:

 >> While NetHack, as a turn-based game, does not suffer from
 >> directly lag-caused deaths, it is still very difficult to
 >> make moves sensibly when the responses are delayed.

Which is probably true in a lot cases, but not generally. (Or
the word "directly" must be considered having a very specific
and restricted meaning here.) If you observe lags of constant
duration that's fine, either you wait long enough or save and
continue later. But if lags appear just sporadically it's bad.
The problem is that I don't think that it is effective enough
to play Nethack if you wait after every keypress for the visual
confirmation in presumed non-critical situations, or wait many
seconds to retype any command if you're unsure whether the key
is still in the input queue or whether you probably just missed
to hit the key effectively.

